<div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 10pt; margin-bottom: 15pt;">
    <h1 style="color: #1e3a8a; margin-bottom: 2pt;">RoadEye™</h1>
    <div style="font-size: 13pt; font-weight: 600; color: #0284c7;">AI-Powered Intelligent Road Monitoring, 3D Cavity Quantification & Municipal Asset Management Platform</div>
    <div style="font-size: 9.5pt; color: #64748b; margin-top: 3pt;">Working Prototype & Live Demo: 114 Passing Tests • GitHub: <a href="https://github.com/haile6/intelligent-road-monitoring-system">haile6/intelligent-road-monitoring-system</a></div>
</div>

<h2>1. Business Idea Title and Description</h2>
<p><strong>Title:</strong> RoadEye™: Edge-AI Road Infrastructure Intelligence & Municipal Asset Management SaaS</p>
<p><strong>Description:</strong> <strong>RoadEye™</strong> is a high-impact, enterprise GovTech and Mobility venture founded at Debre Berhan University. We convert regular, low-cost monocular cameras (dashcams, smartphones, and municipal patrol vehicle cameras) into autonomous, AI-driven road survey systems.</p>
<p>Powered by customized edge computer vision (YOLOv8/11 deep learning), photogrammetric 3D depth geometry, spatial GIS kinematics, and physics-informed deterioration forecasting, RoadEye™ completely eliminates manual, slow, and subjective road inspections. In real-time, the platform detects pavement distresses (potholes, alligator cracking, rutting), estimates 3D cavity depth and required asphalt volume in kilograms, projects hazards on an interactive GIS municipal map, and enforces contractor road warranties (under FIDIC Clause 11 Defect Liability Period).</p>
<p>By identifying road failures before the heavy Ethiopian monsoon (Kiremt) season accelerates their degradation by <strong>4.2&times;</strong>, RoadEye™ enables Debre Berhan City Administration and road authorities to transition from expensive post-collapse emergency reconstruction to proactive, cost-saving preventive maintenance&mdash;<strong>saving municipalities up to 75% in annual road maintenance expenditures.</strong></p>

<h2>2. Problem or Market Need Addressed</h2>
<p>Ethiopian municipalities, the Ethiopian Roads Administration (ERA), and regional transport corridors (particularly the high-volume Route A2 Addis Ababa&ndash;Debre Berhan&ndash;Dessie corridor) suffer from a severe, costly infrastructure crisis:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Dangerous & Manual Pavement Inspections:</strong> Currently, municipal road audits rely on technicians walking along busy highways with measuring wheels and clipboards. This process is lethally dangerous, subjective, covers less than 5% of the road network annually, and takes months to compile.</li>
    <li><strong>Catastrophic Rainy Season (Kiremt) Degradation:</strong> Rain is the number one destroyer of asphalt in the Ethiopian highlands. When water enters unsealed hairline cracks and potholes, heavy truck axle loads create hydrodynamic pore-water pumping that strips bitumen from aggregates. During Kiremt (June&ndash;September), road damage accelerates <strong>4.2&times; faster</strong> than dry season baseline. Small cracks expand into vehicle-swallowing craters within 2 weeks.</li>
    <li><strong>Severe Public Fund Losses from Unenforced Contractor Warranties:</strong> When new roads are constructed, contractors are legally bound under <strong>FIDIC Clause 11 (Defect Liability Period - DLP)</strong> to repair defects at their own expense for 1 to 2 years. Due to the lack of automated tracking, municipal offices fail to document defects before warranty expiration. As a result, municipal taxpayers end up paying tens of millions of ETB for repairs that contractors were legally obligated to fix.</li>
    <li><strong>High Traffic Accidents & Economic Losses:</strong> Substandard road surfaces and sudden, deep potholes are major contributing factors to rollover accidents, tire blowouts, and vehicle suspension damage in Debre Berhan, leading to tragic loss of lives and hundreds of millions of ETB in freight delays.</li>
    <li><strong>Prohibitive Cost of Imported Survey Vehicles:</strong> Traditional laser survey vans (e.g., Dynatest, ROMDAS) cost between <strong>$250,000 and $500,000 USD</strong> to purchase and maintain&mdash;completely unaffordable for Ethiopian municipal governments facing severe foreign currency shortages.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. Proposed Product, Service, or Solution</h2>
<p>RoadEye™ delivers an affordable, end-to-end, locally engineered digital ecosystem consisting of three integrated layers:</p>
<div class="highlight-box">
    <strong>RoadEye™ 3-Tier Architecture:</strong><br/>
    1. <strong>Edge Mobile Sensor Layer:</strong> Low-cost dashcam or smartphone running real-time 30+ FPS YOLOv8 perception.<br/>
    2. <strong>Physics & Geometry Engine:</strong> Monocular 3D depth estimator (cm), truncated paraboloid cavity volume (Liters), asphalt patch mix mass (kg & 25kg bags), and Markov deterioration forecasting.<br/>
    3. <strong>Enterprise Web GIS Platform:</strong> Real-time GIS municipal dashboard, ASTM D6433 Pavement Condition Index (PCI), contractor warranty tracking, and automated legal rectification demand notices.
</div>
<ul>
    <li><strong>High-Accuracy Edge AI Perception:</strong> Real-time detection of potholes and alligator cracking under challenging Ethiopian lighting, shadows, and weather conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>3D Volumetric Cavity & Material Quantification:</strong> Rather than just drawing a 2D box, RoadEye calculates pothole depth (cm), cavity volume (Liters), and exact asphalt cold/hot-mix patching mass (kg and 25kg bags) according to Ethiopian Roads Administration (ERA) specifications.</li>
    <li><strong>Multi-Vehicle Bayesian Fusion:</strong> Fleet crowdsourcing clusters observations from multiple vehicles within 8 meters, eliminating duplicate reports and achieving 99.9% statistical confidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Contractor Warranty Liability Engine (FIDIC Clause 11):</strong> Automatically cross-references detected defects with contractor geofences and active warranty periods. It computes total contractor financial liability based on customizable municipal rates (e.g., 28,000 ETB per pothole, 18,000 ETB per crack cluster) and outputs instant formal rectification demand notices.</li>
    <li><strong>Rainy Season (Kiremt) Cost of Inaction (COI) ROI Engine:</strong> Employs a 5-state Markov chain model adjusted for Ethiopian climate regimes to prove how spending 2,850 ETB/m² on proactive sealing today prevents 7,800 ETB/m² emergency reconstruction in 90 days (delivering an <strong>8.5&times; to 11.2&times; return on investment</strong>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Target Customers and Market Potential</h2>
<h3>4.1 Target Customer Segments</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Municipalities & City Administrations (GovTech B2G):</strong> Debre Berhan City Administration (Infrastructure and Construction Department), Zonal and Regional Urban Planning Bureaus (Amhara Region, Oromia, Addis Ababa).</li>
    <li><strong>National Highway Authorities:</strong> Ethiopian Roads Administration (ERA) &ndash; Directorate of Road Asset Management and Maintenance.</li>
    <li><strong>General Road Construction Contractors (B2B):</strong> Major civil contractors (Defence Construction Enterprise, Sur Construction, Midroc, Yencomad, regional road builders) needing automated pre-handover and warranty inspection tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Public Transport & Logistics Fleets:</strong> Bus fleets, freight operators on Route A2, and logistics companies requiring active in-cabin collision alerts and optimal road-quality routing.</li>
    <li><strong>Automotive Insurance Underwriters:</strong> Risk assessment data to settle vehicular suspension/tire damage claims and verify accident causation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.2 Market Potential</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Total Addressable Market (TAM):</strong> Over <strong>160,000 km</strong> of classified federal and municipal road networks across Ethiopia, with an annual national road maintenance and rehabilitation budget exceeding <strong>25 Billion ETB</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Serviceable Available Market (SAM):</strong> Amhara Region & Central Highway Corridors covering <strong>18,500+ km</strong>, representing an estimated annual inspection and condition monitoring market of <strong>180 Million ETB</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Immediate Serviceable Obtainable Market (SOM):</strong> Debre Berhan City road network (180+ km paved roads) and the adjacent 130 km Debre Berhan&ndash;Addis Ababa freight corridor, with an immediate addressable annual value of <strong>12 to 18 Million ETB</strong>.</li>
</ul>

